Description: Modern container and virtual machine manager - extra tools
 Incus is a modern system container, application container and virtual
 machine manager. It provides a cloud-like environment, creating instances
 from premade images or any Docker (OCI) registry and offers a wide variety
 of features, including the ability to seamlessly cluster servers together.
 .
 It supports multiple different local or remote storage options, traditional
 or fully distributed networking and offers most common cloud features,
 including a full REST API and integrations with common tooling like Ansible,
 Terraform/OpenTofu, Packr, and Kubernetes Cluster API.
 .
 This package contains extra tools provided with Incus.
  - fuidshift - A tool to map/unmap filesystem uids/gids
  - lxc-to-incus - A tool to migrate LXC containers to Incus
  - incus-benchmark - A benchmarking tool for Incus
  - incus-migrate - A tool to turn any filesystem into an Incus instance
  - incus-simplestreams - Maintain an Incus-compatible simplestreams tree

Package: incus-agent
Architecture: linux-any
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Built-Using: ${misc:Built-Using}
Static-Built-Using: ${misc:Static-Built-Using}
Description: Modern container and virtual machine manager - VM guest agent
 Incus is a modern system container, application container and virtual
 machine manager. It provides a cloud-like environment, creating instances
 from premade images or any Docker (OCI) registry and offers a wide variety
 of features, including the ability to seamlessly cluster servers together.
 .
 It supports multiple different local or remote storage options, traditional
 or fully distributed networking and offers most common cloud features,
 including a full REST API and integrations with common tooling like Ansible,
 Terraform/OpenTofu, Packr, and Kubernetes Cluster API.
 .
 This package provides an agent to run inside Incus virtual machines.
 Currently supported guest operating systems include Linux, FreeBSD, MacOS,
 and Windows.
 .
 It has to be installed on the Incus host if you want to allow agent
 injection capability when creating a virtual machine.
